The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (bp->ptp_clock)
    ptp_clock_unregister(bp->ptp_clock);
```
```c
ptp_clock_index(bp->ptp_clock); // in gem_get_ts_info()
```
Reason:
The PTP clock pointer `bp->ptp_clock` is not set to `NULL` after being freed by `ptp_clock_unregister()`. A dangling pointer remains. Later, an ethtool `get_ts_info` call (via `gem_get_ts_info()`) dereferences this pointer and passes it to `ptp_clock_index()`, which accesses already freed memory, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (bp->ptp_clock) {
    ptp_clock_unregister(bp->ptp_clock);
    bp->ptp_clock = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
Setting `bp->ptp_clock = NULL` after unregistering the clock ensures that the pointer no longer points to freed memory. Any subsequent access to `bp->ptp_clock` (e.g., in `get_ts_info`) will encounter a `NULL` pointer, which either prevents the use-after-free or is handled by existing NULL checks.

